DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       The ssttrrssiiggnnaall() function returns a string describing the signal  number
       passed in the argument _s_i_g.  The string can be used only until the next
       call  to  ssttrrssiiggnnaall().  The string returned by ssttrrssiiggnnaall() is localized
       according to the LLCC__MMEESSSSAAGGEESS category in the current locale.

       The ssiiggddeessccrr__nnpp() function returns a string describing the signal  num‐
       ber  passed in the argument _s_i_g.  Unlike ssttrrssiiggnnaall() this string is not
       influenced by the current locale.

       The ssiiggaabbbbrreevv__nnpp() function returns the abbreviated name of the signal,
       _s_i_g.  For example, given the value SSIIGGIINNTT, it returns the string "INT".

       The (deprecated) array _s_y_s___s_i_g_l_i_s_t holds the signal description strings
       indexed by signal number.  The ssttrrssiiggnnaall() or the  ssiiggddeessccrr__nnpp()  func‐
       tion should be used instead of this array; see also VERSIONS.

RREETTUURRNN VVAALLUUEE
       The ssttrrssiiggnnaall() function returns the appropriate description string, or
       an  unknown  signal  message  if the signal number is invalid.  On some
       systems (but not on Linux), NULL may instead be returned for an invalid
       signal number.

       The ssiiggddeessccrr__nnpp() and ssiiggaabbbbrreevv__nnpp() functions return  the  appropriate
       description  string.   The  returned string is statically allocated and
       valid for the lifetime of the program.  These functions return NULL for
       an invalid signal number.

